her_cause":false},{"text":"psychosis","hpo_label":"Psychosis","hpo_id":"HP:0000709","present":true,"n_with":null,"n_assessed":null,"some_only":false,"other_cause":false},{"text":"irritable bowel syndrome","hpo_label":"","hpo_id":"","present":true,"n_with":null,"n_assessed":null,"some_only":false,"other_cause":false},{"text":"mild splenomegaly","hpo_label":"Splenomegaly","hpo_id":"HP:0001744","present":true,"n_with":null,"n_assessed":null,"some_only":false,"other_cause":false},{"text":"recurrent otitis media","hpo_label":"Recurrent otitis media","hpo_id":"HP:0000403","present":true,"n_with":null,"n_assessed":null,"some_only":false,"other_cause":false},{"text":"recurrent sinusitis","hpo_label":"Recurrent sinusitis","hpo_id":"HP:0011108","present":true,"n_with":null,"n_assessed":null,"some_only":false,"other_cause":false},{"text":"shingles","hpo_label":"Unusual VZV skin infection","hpo_id":"HP:5210235","present":true,"n_with":null,"n_assessed":null,"some_only":false,"other_cause":false},{"text":"low serum IgM","hpo_label":"Decreased circulating IgM concentration","hpo_id":"HP:0002850","present":true,"n_with":null,"n_assessed":null,"some_only":false,"other_cause":false},{"text":"low isohemagglutinins","hpo_label":"Decreased circulating isohemagglutinin concentration","hpo_id":"HP:0032139","present":true,"n_with":null,"n_assessed":null,"some_only":false,"other_cause":false},{"text":"T cell lymphopenia","hpo_label":"Decreased total T cell count","hpo_id":"HP:0005403","present":true,"n_with":null,"n_assessed":null,"some_only":false,"other_cause":false}],"variants":[{"gene":"GNAI2","as_reported":"Arg179His","cdna":"","protein":"p.Arg179His","genomic":"","zygosity":"heterozygous","origin":"unknown","consequence":"missense","key":"V3"}],"variant_roles":["causal"],"segregation":"Shared with affected brother (P18)","inheritance":"AD","previously_reported":"","mixed_genotypes":false,"quotes":["His deceased brother P19 had dysmorphism (micrognathia, high anterior hairline, low-set ears), cryptorchidism, inguinal hernia, deviated nasal septum, IUGR with childhood failure to thrive and adult short stature, neurodevelopmental delay, epilepsy, ataxia, nystagmus, migraine headaches, autism spectrum disorder","irritable bowel syndrome, mild splenomegaly, recurrent infections (otitis media, pneumonia, bronchitis, sinusitis, shingles), dysgammaglobulinemia (low serum IgM, low isohemagglutinins, and variably low vaccine titers), and T cell lymphopenia."],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0],"followup_of":"","genotype_mode":"AD","genotype_reason":"single heterozygous variant; mode as stated for the family"}],"notes":[{"id":"N:40926810:1","pmid":"40926810","section":"description","statement":"Lamborn et al. (2025) reviewed MAGIS syndrome, an acronym for midline malformations of the brain, anterior pituitary gland dysfunction, growth retardation, immunodysregulation/immunodeficiency, and skeletal defects. It is caused by heterozygous germline activating (gain-of-function) mutations in GNAI2. Nearly all patients had both immune and nonimmune features, but severity varied.","inheritance":"AD","quotes":["This syndrome has now been named MAGIS, an acronym that denotes five cardinal syndromic features: Midline malformations of the brain, Anterior pituitary gland dysfunction, Growth retardation, Immunodysregulation/Immunodeficiency, and Skeletal defects (Fig. 1).","Broadly, nearly all patients display both immune and nonimmune disease features, but with variable severity."],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0]},{"id":"N:40926810:2","pmid":"40926810","section":"clinical_features","statement":"In a cohort of 20 patients from 18 families, Lamborn et al. (2025) found growth retardation in 90%. This included IUGR (53%) and postnatal short stature (90%). Eight of 11 tested patients had low IGF1, and all 7 formally tested had growth hormone deficiency. Fifty-nine percent had structural or functional anterior pituitary defects, 30% had midline brain malformations, 85% had skeletal dysostoses, and all had dysmorphia.","inheritance":"UNKNOWN","quotes":["Among these, growth retardation is most penetrant (90%), manifesting as both prenatal intrauterine growth restriction (IUGR; 53%) and postnatal short stature (90%), as well as low serum insulin-like growth factor-1 (8 of 11 tested, 73%) and growth hormone deficiency (7 of 7 formally tested).","structural (41%) or functional (53%) defects of the anterior pituitary gland are present in 59% of patients","Skeletal defects comprise diverse craniofacial, appendicular, and axial skeletal dysostoses affecting 85% of patients and underlie the high prevalence of dysmorphia (100%)."],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0,1.0]},{"id":"N:40926810:3","pmid":"40926810","section":"clinical_features","statement":"Lamborn et al. (2025) reported immune-mediated disease in 95% of the cohort, with immunodeficiency in 90% and immune dysregulation in 50%. Infections were mainly sinopulmonary, and 25% had cutaneous viral infections, primarily warts. Autoimmunity (35%) included autoimmune hemolytic anemia (20%), autoimmune thrombocytopenia (10%), and psoriasis (10%). Premature mortality was 10%.","inheritance":"UNKNOWN","quotes":["Immune-mediated disease (95%) is widely present in MAGIS as both immunodeficiency (90%) and immune dysregulation (50%; systemic autoinflammation, 15%, autoimmunity, 35%, and splenomegaly, 35%).","Autoimmune manifestations included life-threatening autoimmune hemolytic anemia (20%), autoimmune thrombocytopenia (10%), psoriasis (10%), and Hashimoto’s thyroiditis, type I diabetes mellitus, alopecia, celiac disease, and autoimmune enteritis in individual patients to date.","While we observed a relatively low premature mortality (10% in our original cohort of 20 patients)"],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0,1.0]},{"id":"N:40926810:4","pmid":"40926810","section":"clinical_features","statement":"Lamborn et al. (2025) noted that lymphocyte immunophenotyping usually showed low to normal T cells, a low naive-to-memory T cell ratio, and low B cells with low serum IgM. Mitogen- or antigen-induced proliferation was normal or increased. Rare features such as coloboma or subcortical band heterotopia should raise suspicion of MAGIS.","inheritance":"UNKNOWN","quotes":["In general, lymphocyte immunophenotyping in peripheral blood demonstrates low/normal T cells often with improvement with age, low naïve-to-memory T cell ratio, and low B cells with associated low serum IgM.","the presence of extremely rare features, such as coloboma or subcortical band heterotopia (double cortex syndrome), should automatically raise suspicion for an underlying MAGIS diagnosis."],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0]},{"id":"N:40926810:5","pmid":"40926810","section":"molecular_genetics","statement":"Lamborn et al. (2025) stated that every patient carried an extremely rare heterozygous missense GNAI2 variant. Variants recurred at thr182 (Thr182Ala/Ile/Pro in 6 families) and arg179 (Arg179His/Cys in 7 patients from 5 families). Most variants arose de novo, but 2 families showed transmission consistent with autosomal dominant inheritance, and penetrance was full in the families studied.","inheritance":"AD","quotes":["MAGIS displayed full disease penetrance in the families we studied, with mutations recurring among unrelated patients often observed at residues Thr182 (Thr182Ala/Ile/Pro in six families) and Arg179 (Arg179His/Cys in seven patients from five families).","Most pathogenic variants occur in a de novo pattern with mutations detected in several tissues, suggesting the de novo variants arose in germ cells or early during embryonic development."],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0]},{"id":"N:40926810:6","pmid":"40926810","section":"population_genetics","statement":"Lamborn et al. (2025) found that all patient variants were absent from population databases except Arg179His (rs137853227). This variant was seen in 2 individuals of unknown affection status in gnomAD v4.1.0 (minor allele frequency 0.000001281).","inheritance":"UNKNOWN","quotes":["All such patient variants are absent from general population genetic databases except for rs137853227 (Arg179His), which was found in two individuals in gnomAD v4.1.0, accessed March 2025 (minor allele frequency 0.000001281, one of African American ancestry and the other of European ancestry), having unknown affection status."],"quote_grounding":[1.0]},{"id":"N:40926810:7","pmid":"40926810","section":"gene_function","statement":"Lamborn et al. (2025) summarised that mutant Galpha-i2 bound GTP up to 20-fold faster and hydrolyzed GTP up to 100-fold slower than wildtype, and that most mutants were insensitive to RGS GAPs. The Arg179His and Arg179Cys mutants were milder: they bound GTP more slowly than wildtype and stayed GAP-sensitive. The authors suggested this explains their transmission across generations.","inheritance":"UNKNOWN","quotes":["Biochemical analysis demonstrated that mutant Gαi2 binds GTP up to 20-fold faster than wild-type (WT) and hydrolyzes GTP up to 100-fold slower than WT Gαi2.","The Arg179His and Arg179Cys Gαi2 proteins bind GTP more slowly than WT Gαi2 and remain sensitive to the GTP hydrolysis–promoting effects of GAPs","These characteristics explain why the Arg179His/Cys variants can be passed on through successive generations"],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0,1.0]},{"id":"N:40926810:8","pmid":"40926810","section":"pathogenesis","statement":"Lamborn et al. (2025) described impaired migration and chemokine-induced calcium flux in patient T cells and neutrophils. Mutant proteins remained largely decoupled from GPCRs, causing constitutive insensitivity to CXCL12-CXCR4 signaling, the opposite of the mechanism in WHIM syndrome. T cell hyperresponsiveness arose because active Galpha-i2 sequestered RASA2, enhancing RAS-ERK and PI3K-AKT-S6 signaling.","inheritance":"UNKNOWN","quotes":["MAGIS mutant proteins remain predominantly decoupled from GPCRs at steady state, consistent with their biochemically activated state.","Thus, mechanistically MAGIS syndrome presents as a CXCR4 signaling deficiency.","Instead of directly inhibiting RASA2’s GAP activity toward RAS, the activating mutant Gαi2 sequesters RASA2 toward the plasma membrane."],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0,1.0]},{"id":"N:40926810:9","pmid":"40926810","section":"pathogenesis","statement":"Lamborn et al. (2025) found that CRISPR knockout of AC3 and AC7 in healthy donor T cells, used to mimic low cAMP, neither impaired chemotaxis nor enhanced activation. The authors suggested that the chemotaxis defect and T cell hyperresponsiveness are independent of cAMP.","inheritance":"UNKNOWN","quotes":["Surprisingly, these manipulations did not impair T cell chemotaxis nor did they enhance T cell activation and proliferation as seen in MAGIS patient cells.","These data suggest the impaired chemotaxis and T cell hyperresponsiveness of MAGIS syndrome are independent of cAMP and related to the mechanisms described above."],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0]},{"id":"N:40926810:10","pmid":"40926810","section":"clinical_management","statement":"Lamborn et al. (2025) reported a patient who achieved full donor chimerism after HSCT from a matched unrelated donor. The transplant eradicated granulomatous skin disease due to vaccine-strain rubella and recurrent infections, and autoimmune hemolytic anemia stayed in remission for over 3 years. Nonimmune features were unaffected. In vitro, combined RAS/ERK/MAPK and PI3K/AKT inhibitors corrected T cell hyperresponsiveness, suggesting agents such as sirolimus or PDE inhibitors as possible therapies.","inheritance":"UNKNOWN","quotes":["HSCT successfully eradicated the patient’s granulomatous skin disease caused by vaccine-strain rubella virus, as well as his recurrent upper respiratory tract infections, otitis media, and bronchitis.","Furthermore, inhibitors of the RAS/ERK/MAPK and PI3K/AKT pathways were each able to partially correct the T cell hyperresponsiveness in vitro, although both inhibitors were required for complete correction","As some of these conditions are observed in MAGIS patients, normalizing their cAMP levels by treating with PDE inhibitors might be considered."],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0,1.0]},{"id":"N:31036916:1","pmid":"31036916","section":"molecular_genetics","statement":"Lecoquierre et al. (2019) searched a database of more than 1,200 solo exomes for missense variants also listed in denovo-db, a public collection of de novo variants from patients with developmental disorders. This found 30 variants with strong evidence of pathogenicity. The authors identified FEM1B and GNAI2 as good candidate genes for syndromic intellectual disability.","inheritance":"UNKNOWN","quotes":["We looked for variations independently present in both our database of >1200 solo exomes and in denovo-db, a large, publicly available collection of de novo variants identified in patients with DD.","We identified FEM1B and GNAI2 as good candidate genes for syndromic intellectual disability and confirmed the implication of ACTL6B in a neurodevelopmental disorder."],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0]},{"id":"N:27787898:1","pmid":"27787898","section":"animal_model","statement":"Hamada et al. (2017) found that acute knockdown of Gnai2 by in utero electroporation in mouse caused delayed radial migration of excitatory cortical neurons, perhaps because of impaired morphology; the defect was rescued by an RNAi-resistant Gnai2. Knockdown did not affect axon elongation, dendritic arbor formation, or neurogenesis at the ventricular zone.","inheritance":"UNKNOWN","quotes":["Acute knockdown of the α-subunit (Gαi2) with in utero electroporation caused delayed radial migration of excitatory neurons during corticogenesis, perhaps because of impaired morphology.","The migration phenotype was rescued by an RNAi-resistant version of Gαi2.","silencing of Gαi2 did not affect axon elongation, dendritic arbor formation or neurogenesis at ventricular zone in vivo"],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0,1.0]},{"id":"N:27787898:2","pmid":"27787898","section":"animal_model","statement":"Hamada et al. (2017) found that mice with acute Gnai2 knockdown showed defects in social interaction, novelty recognition, and active avoidance learning, as well as increased anxiety.","inheritance":"UNKNOWN","quotes":["When behavior analyses were conducted with acute Gαi2-knockdown mice, they showed defects in social interaction, novelty recognition and active avoidance learning as well as increased anxiety."],"quote_grounding":[1.0]}],"dropped":{"records":[],"notes":[]},"gene_decisions":{"candidates":["GNAI2"],"confirmed":["GNAI2"],"dropped":[],"not_confirmed":[]},"candidate_inheritance":["AD"]}